 \subsection{Preprocessing and model selection}
 Information can enter a model before the final training step. Consider sensor scaling. A model may express each reading as a distance from the average training value. If that average includes the held-out experiment, the test measurements have already influenced the values given to the model. The same issue applies when filling gaps, selecting features, tuning a model, adjusting probabilities, or choosing a decision threshold. These steps need to use the development data allowed for that split \parencite{P017}. Reusing evaluation results to select a configuration can make its reported performance look too favourable \parencite{P004}.
-The PHM records contain a concrete example. The principal sensor experiment fitted its normaliser again inside each training fold. A separate four-model diagnostic fitted the normaliser before the program changed which gear experiment was held out. In that diagnostic, measurements from the held-out experiment could affect scaling. The two procedures are reported separately. Measuring how much this changed the diagnostic result would require running it again with scaling fitted inside each fold.
+The separate PHM four-arm diagnostic illustrates this risk; Section~\ref{sec:phm-diagnostics} distinguishes its shared normalizer from the fold-local preprocessing of the principal sensor comparison.
 A frozen image encoder presents a different case. If its weights were learned from an external dataset and its feature extraction uses no target labels or statistics from the assessment population, the fixed image features can be saved in advance. Later steps that \emph{learn} from those features still have to respect the training split.
 \subsection{Training the REASSEMBLE stacker}
 The REASSEMBLE stacker learned from two numbers for each training action: a camera score and a sensor score. It needed scores like the ones it would receive for an unfamiliar recording. A score from a branch that had already trained on the same recording could give the stacker an unrealistically easy example.
